# A fixture-backed example of a mutating operation: restart one exact systemd
# unit.
#
# This file is an executable syntax example, not a safe default operation.
# Run it directly only when `yesh-codex-fixture.service` is the disposable
# unit created by `scripts/yesh-fixture-test.sh`. Otherwise inspect it with:
#
#   ./yesh --plan examples/service-restart.yesh
#
# Do not replace the fixture name with a production unit and run it casually:
# `service restart` changes system state. A real production restart requires
# an explicit operational decision, appropriate privileges, and a service-
# specific readiness check after this statement.
#
# `systemctl restart` only tells us that systemd accepted and completed the
# restart job. The yesh operation therefore requires a postcondition and
# polls the unit before reporting success. The restart diagnostic also
# records Result, ExecMainCode, ExecMainStatus and the monotonic active-entry
# timestamp; those fields explain a failure but do not prove application
# readiness.
#
# `within 30s` is one finite client budget for restart and postcondition
# polling. It is deliberately not `systemctl --wait`: --wait can wait for a
# long-lived service to terminate rather than confirm that it is active.
#
# The unit name is explicit. A name without `.service`, paths,
# globs and shell operators are rejected; yesh must not silently broaden the
# target selected by the author.
#
# Bash equivalent (less safe to review):
#   systemctl restart yesh-codex-fixture.service && \
#     test "$(systemctl show -p Id --value yesh-codex-fixture.service)" = yesh-codex-fixture.service && \
#     test "$(systemctl show -p ActiveState --value yesh-codex-fixture.service)" = active
# The yesh form keeps argv boundaries, timeout and the postcondition visible.
# It does not add `sudo`, enable the unit, or repair a failed service.
# Role of yesh: keep the service or journal request bounded and visible; systemd remains the source of state.

service restart yesh-codex-fixture.service until active within 10s
